the Village Hall from 10.30am to 12.30.&nbsp;</p><p>Everybody is guaranteed a warm welcome!&nbsp;</p><p>Hot drinks, biscuits and cake will be provided for a small contribution to cover costs (suggested donations: £1 for a drink and £1- £1.50 for a piece of cake).&nbsp;</p><p>If you’d like to bake biscuits or a cake, please let Daniela Jones know (<a href="mailto:daniela.lynchets@gmail.com">daniela.lynchets@gmail.com</a>) and you can easily be reimbursed for the ingredients.</p><p>These coffee mornings are being held on the first Saturday of every month for a chance to see old friends and meet new people.&nbsp;</p><p>It might be especially nice for newcomers who have not had many chances to meet other villagers yet.&nbsp;</p><p>So, please come and do spread the word!&nbsp;</p><p>As parking is limited, please come on foot if at all possible.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('event','0','2070','2166','en','0','<p>The first Saturday of the month means that it’s time to pop into the village hall for the popular Repair Café with skilled sewers, electrical bods, general repairers and computer advice.&nbsp;</p><p>Successful repairs have included:&nbsp;kettles, jumpers, table lamps,&nbsp;garden tools, kitchen knives, wonky tables, picture frames, sports bags and more.&nbsp;</p><p>As well as repairs, many IT queries have been sorted: helping people keep their technology working better and for longer, or giving advice on the best courses of action.&nbsp;</p><p>If you have something that you would like advice on, or something that you’re not sure if it could be repaired, bring it along and the team of repairers will see what can be done.</p><p>With all the usual hot drinks, delicious cakes and warm welcome from the monthly coffee morning team.</p>','','full_html'),('news','0','2071','2167','en','0','<p>The audience in the village hall was treated to a fascinating illustrated talk in March about one of the parish’s most remarkable natural treasures: our dark skies.</p><p>Elinor Newman of the South Downs National Park Authority — affectionately known as the “Queen of Darkness” — gave a lively and enlightening presentation about Buriton’s night skies and the many creatures that emerge after sunset but are rarely seen.</p><p>She reminded everyone that the South Downs became an International Dark Sky Reserve in May 2016, following three years of dedicated work, and that Buriton holds a special place within the Reserve, with two Dark Sky Discovery Sites right on our doorstep.&nbsp;</p><p>Few places offer such a wonderful opportunity to enjoy nature’s finest free spectacle: sparkling stars, bright planets and the sudden thrill of a shooting star streaking overhead.</p><p>The talk also shone a light on the rich nocturnal wildlife that depends on darkness, from bats and owls to glow worms, moths, hedgehogs, frogs and toads. Elinor shared a host of memorable facts about these species, explaining how darkness supports feeding, movement, mating and migration.</p><p>A key message was that darkness matters for wildlife as much as daylight does. Light pollution can disrupt life cycles, disturb feeding corridors and even affect daytime creatures by depriving them of proper rest.</p><p>Encouragingly, there are simple steps everyone can take to help: close curtains, switch lights off when not needed, use modest wattages, and ensure outdoor lighting is downward-facing, shielded and only on when necessary.</p><p>Those attending left both entertained and inspired, with practical ideas for helping protect Buriton’s special night-time environment.</p><p>More tips (and more information about star-gazing) can be found in a special leaflet produced for Buriton: <a href="http://www.buriton.info/sites/default/files/2017-09/Dark%20Skies%20-%20Buriton%20information%20leaflet.pdf">Explore the Dark Side of Buriton</a>&nbsp;</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('event','0','2073','2169','en','0','<p>Buriton’s Local History project has been collecting old photographs, memories and other information about life in the parish for many years.&nbsp;</p><p>This exhibition is a chance to see some of what has been discovered – and perhaps help add another piece to the story.</p><p>There will be a wide range of displays, photographs and information about life in the parish in years gone by – and copies of all Buriton’s Local History books will also be available.</p><p>The exhibition is free and will take place in the village hall on Saturday 26 September, from 1pm to 4pm.</p><p>This year marks the 50th anniversary of the Queen Elizabeth Country Park, and the&nbsp;exhibition will include information about the history of the park, including its opening by Her Late Majesty Queen Elizabeth II.</p><p>And there will be evidence of the thousands of years of history that lie under our feet – from the Bronze Age, Celtic and Roman times through to the Civil War and beyond. &nbsp;The exhibition will include an intriguing collection of finds uncovered by local historians which give clues about what life was like around Buriton long ago. Ancient coins, brooches, buckles, thimbles and buttons may be small, but each offers a glimpse into the lives of people who lived, worked and travelled through the area centuries ago.</p><p>Other displays will include:</p><ul type="disc"><li>Hop growing and picking – the seasonal activity that once transformed the parish.</li><li>The Buriton and Butser chalk quarries and lime works – a reminder of another important local industry.</li><li>The railway – including local families who worked on the line.</li><li>Leisure and entertainment – from football and cricket to pheasant and rabbit shoots, early entertainments and social clubs.</li></ul><p>A particular highlight will be a fascinating collection of nearly 100 historic village postcards, some more than a century old, all enlarged to A3 size.</p><p>Entry is free and everyone is encouraged to bring any old photos, newspaper articles or other information relating to Buriton, Weston, Nursted or Ditcham.</p><p>So come along, explore the displays, share your memories and see how much you recognise. &nbsp;It promises to be another fascinating trip down memory lane!</p><p>If you can help with the research in any way, please contact Doug Jones on 01730 231326 or visit <a href="http://www.buriton.org.uk/">www.buriton.org.uk</a></p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('event','0','2074','2170','en','0','<p>Another wonderful concert in St Mary''s church.</p><p>Music for piano by Franz Schubert</p><p>Played by Tom Cooper</p><p>There will be light refreshments after the concert</p><p>And a retiring collection in aid of the work of St Mary''s</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2078','2175','en','0','<p>Stroll round the village to see how the community is helping nature.</p><p>An opportunity to visit a range of gardens and public open spaces&nbsp;around the village to see how they are catering for wildlife.</p><p>Each garden will show some simple renaturing initiatives to help creatures and the planet. Ask each of the hosts for details.</p><p>There are lots of wonderful plants and creations to see - as well as Cream Teas and Cakes at St Mary''s church (2-5pm).&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>Details of the trail (with a map showing all the locations) will be available from the church.</p><p>It’s hoped that the gardens will inspire everyone to join the community’s campaign to support nature and improve life for all just by doing some very simple things in your own back yard.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2079','2176','en','0','<p>The first Cream Tea of the year in the church.</p><p>All proceeds in aid of St Mary''s.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2085','2182','en','0','<p>The next in the series of coffee mornings will be held in the Village Hall from 10.30am to 12.30.&nbsp;</p><p>Everybody is guaranteed a warm welcome!&nbsp;</p><p>Hot drinks, biscuits and cake will be provided for a small contribution to cover costs (suggested donations: £1 for a drink and £1- £1.50 for a piece of cake).&nbsp;</p><p>If you’d like to bake biscuits or a cake, please let Daniela Jones know (<a href="mailto:daniela.lynchets@gmail.com">daniela.lynchets@gmail.com</a>) and you can easily be reimbursed for the ingredients.</p><p>These coffee mornings are being held on the first Saturday of every month for a chance to see old friends and meet new people.&nbsp;</p><p>It might be especially nice for newcomers who have not had many chances to meet other villagers yet.&nbsp;</p><p>So, please come and do spread the word!&nbsp;</p><p>As parking is limited, please come on foot if at all possible.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('event','0','2086','2183','en','0','<p>The next in the series of coffee mornings will be held in the Village Hall from 10.30am to 12.30.&nbsp;</p><p>Everybody is guaranteed a warm welcome!&nbsp;</p><p>Hot drinks, biscuits and cake will be provided for a small contribution to cover costs (suggested donations: £1 for a drink and £1- £1.50 for a piece of cake).&nbsp;</p><p>If you’d like to bake biscuits or a cake, please let Daniela Jones know (<a href="mailto:daniela.lynchets@gmail.com">daniela.lynchets@gmail.com</a>) and you can easily be reimbursed for the ingredients.</p><p>These coffee mornings are being held on the first Saturday of every month for a chance to see old friends and meet new people.&nbsp;</p><p>It might be especially nice for newcomers who have not had many chances to meet other villagers yet.&nbsp;</p><p>So, please come and do spread the word!&nbsp;</p><p>As parking is limited, please come on foot if at all possible.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2087','2184','en','0','<p>The first Saturday of the month means that it’s time to pop into the village hall for the popular Repair Café with skilled sewers, electrical bods, general repairers and computer advice.&nbsp;</p><p>Successful repairs have included:&nbsp;kettles, jumpers, table lamps,&nbsp;garden tools, kitchen knives, wonky tables, picture frames, sports bags and more.&nbsp;</p><p>As well as repairs, many IT queries have been sorted: helping people keep their technology working better and for longer, or giving advice on the best courses of action.&nbsp;</p><p>If you have something that you would like advice on, or something that you’re not sure if it could be repaired, bring it along and the team of repairers will see what can be done.</p><p>With all the usual hot drinks, delicious cakes and warm welcome from the monthly coffee morning team.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2088','2185','en','0','<p>The next in the series of coffee mornings will be held in the Village Hall from 10.30am to 12.30.&nbsp;</p><p>Everybody is guaranteed a warm welcome!&nbsp;</p><p>Hot drinks, biscuits and cake will be provided for a small contribution to cover costs (suggested donations: £1 for a drink and £1- £1.50 for a piece of cake).&nbsp;</p><p>If you’d like to bake biscuits or a cake, please let Daniela Jones know (<a href="mailto:daniela.lynchets@gmail.com">daniela.lynchets@gmail.com</a>) and you can easily be reimbursed for the ingredients.</p><p>These coffee mornings are being held on the first Saturday of every month for a chance to see old friends and meet new people.&nbsp;</p><p>It might be especially nice for newcomers who have not had many chances to meet other villagers yet.&nbsp;</p><p>So, please come and do spread the word!&nbsp;</p><p>As parking is limited, please come on foot if at all possible.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('event','0','2089','2186','en','0','<p>The first Saturday of the month means that it’s time to pop into the village hall for the popular Repair Café with skilled sewers, electrical bods, general repairers and computer advice.&nbsp;</p><p>Successful repairs have included:&nbsp;kettles, jumpers, table lamps,&nbsp;garden tools, kitchen knives, wonky tables, picture frames, sports bags and more.&nbsp;</p><p>As well as repairs, many IT queries have been sorted: helping people keep their technology working better and for longer, or giving advice on the best courses of action.&nbsp;</p><p>If you have something that you would like advice on, or something that you’re not sure if it could be repaired, bring it along and the team of repairers will see what can be done.</p><p>With all the usual hot drinks, delicious cakes and warm welcome from the monthly coffee morning team.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2090','2187','en','0','<p>The next in the series of coffee mornings will be held in the Village Hall from 10.30am to 12.30.&nbsp;</p><p>Everybody is guaranteed a warm welcome!&nbsp;</p><p>Hot drinks, biscuits and cake will be provided for a small contribution to cover costs (suggested donations: £1 for a drink and £1- £1.50 for a piece of cake).&nbsp;</p><p>If you’d like to bake biscuits or a cake, please let Daniela Jones know (<a href="mailto:daniela.lynchets@gmail.com">daniela.lynchets@gmail.com</a>) and you can easily be reimbursed for the ingredients.</p><p>These coffee mornings are being held on the first Saturday of every month for a chance to see old friends and meet new people.&nbsp;</p><p>It might be especially nice for newcomers who have not had many chances to meet other villagers yet.&nbsp;</p><p>So, please come and do spread the word!&nbsp;</p><p>As parking is limited, please come on foot if at all possible.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2091','2188','en','0','<p>The first Saturday of the month means that it’s time to pop into the village hall for the popular Repair Café with skilled sewers, electrical bods, general repairers and computer advice.&nbsp;</p><p>Successful repairs have included:&nbsp;kettles, jumpers, table lamps,&nbsp;garden tools, kitchen knives, wonky tables, picture frames, sports bags and more.&nbsp;</p><p>As well as repairs, many IT queries have been sorted: helping people keep their technology working better and for longer, or giving advice on the best courses of action.&nbsp;</p><p>If you have something that you would like advice on, or something that you’re not sure if it could be repaired, bring it along and the team of repairers will see what can be done.</p><p>With all the usual hot drinks, delicious cakes and warm welcome from the monthly coffee morning team.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('event','0','2092','2189','en','0','<p>The first Saturday of the month means that it’s time to pop into the village hall for the popular Repair Café with skilled sewers, electrical bods, general repairers and computer advice.&nbsp;</p><p>Successful repairs have included:&nbsp;kettles, jumpers, table lamps,&nbsp;garden tools, kitchen knives, wonky tables, picture frames, sports bags and more.&nbsp;</p><p>As well as repairs, many IT queries have been sorted: helping people keep their technology working better and for longer, or giving advice on the best courses of action.&nbsp;</p><p>If you have something that you would like advice on, or something that you’re not sure if it could be repaired, bring it along and the team of repairers will see what can be done.</p><p>With all the usual hot drinks, delicious cakes and warm welcome from the monthly coffee morning team.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2093','2190','en','0','<p>Will include a variety of repertoire from the Great American Song book, Jazz standards and compositions from his favourite composers such as Thelonius Monk, Duke Ellington and Billy Strayhorn.&nbsp;</p><p>Light refreshments after the concert and a retiring collection in aid of St Mary’s.</p><p>A wonderful way to spend a summer Sunday afternoon</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2094','2191','en','0','<p>All proceeds in aid of St Mary''s Church.</p><p>There will also be Cream Teas on Sunday 6<sup>th</sup> September (2pm)&nbsp;</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2095','2192','en','0','<p>All proceeds in aid of St Mary''s Church.</p>','','full_html'),('news','0','2096','2193','en','0','<p>Glorious sunshine helped make this year''s Buriton Open Gardens Renaturing Trail a great success, with scores of visitors enjoying the seven venues around the village.&nbsp;</p><p>The Trail combined a variety of private gardens with public open spaces, providing plenty of inspiration about how everyone can play a part in supporting nature.</p><p>The gardens showed how native hedges, rather than fences, can provide food and shelter for a range of creatures, particularly birds and important pollinators.</p><p>And parts of each lawn left unmown from spring through to autumn had become havens for wildflowers, colourful grasses, orchids, primroses and knapweeds.</p><p>Even the regularly mown parts of each lawn were being cut at a slightly higher level, allowing clover to flourish and providing a valuable source of nectar for bees.</p><p>Small ponds were teeming with frogs, newts, pond skaters and water boatmen, while dragonflies and damselflies were frequent visitors.&nbsp;</p><p>Garden owners explained how sharing their gardens with wildlife had brought an extra sense of enjoyment, delight and discovery throughout the seasons.</p><p>Visitors also saw how the Recreation Ground''s wild borders are now buzzing with butterflies, bees and moths amongst swathes of yellow rattle, selfheal, oxeye daisies, knapweed, buttercups and clover.</p><p>The Trail also highlighted the work taking place over the last year to enhance biodiversity at the village pond, with hopes that improvements can also be made on the District Council’s nearby pond green area, too. Together with parts of the churchyard, these areas provide important wildlife corridors, helping pollinators and other species do their work to help produce food for us all to eat.</p><p>Inside the church, display boards described some of the excellent work being carried out by Buriton''s farmers to support nature, including creating new ponds, restoring chalk downland, planting wildlife-friendly hedgerows, improving the breeding success of important birds and providing support for endangered species.</p><p>Visitors could also learn practical ways to help hedgehogs and garden birds at home.</p><p>It’s hoped that the event will inspire more people to join the village’s campaign to support nature. More information&nbsp;can be obtained by emailing&nbsp;<a href="mailto:buritonbea@gmail.com">buritonbea@gmail.com</a>&nbsp;as well as by visiting the ‘Helping Nature’ parts of the community website:&nbsp;<a href="https://www.buriton.info/helping-nature-help-us">Helping nature help us | Buriton Community Website</a></p><p>Overall, it was another enjoyable and inspiring event, showing how even small changes in gardens and green spaces can make a real difference for wildlife.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('event','0','2100','2197','en','0','<p>Local artists will be staging an exhibition of Arts and Crafts in the village hall on both Sunday and Bank Holiday Monday (30<sup>th</sup> and 31<sup>st</sup> August) from 10am to 4pm</p><p>There will be decorative and functional pottery, fused glass, giclee prints, lino prints, cards, photography, original paintings and animal portraiture.</p><p>It’s just for these two days.</p><p>Delicious refreshments served throughout.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2101','2198','en','0','<p>Local artists will be staging an exhibition of Arts and Crafts in the village hall on both Sunday and Bank Holiday Monday (30<sup>th</sup> and 31<sup>st</sup> August) from 10am to 4pm</p><p>There will be decorative and functional pottery, fused glass, giclee prints, lino prints, cards, photography, original paintings and animal portraiture.</p><p>It’s just for these two days.</p><p>Delicious refreshments served throughout.</p>','','full_html'),('news','0','2102','2199','en','0','<p>During hot, dry spells Hampshire’s Fire &amp; Rescue Service is worried about wildfires which can spread quickly to village homes and gardens.</p><p>With fields being tinder dry it is feared that any spark could create a Buriton version of fires elsewhere in the country.</p><p>There have already been serious fires elsewhere in Hampshire this summer as well as in Sussex, Somerset, Devon, Lancashire, Derbyshire, Durham and North Wales.</p><p>Winds can easily spread embers to homes and gardens some distance from any blaze. So, the risk of fires can affect everyone.</p><p>As well as refraining from having bonfires, there are other tips from the authorities:&nbsp;</p><ul><li>opt for a picnic rather than a barbecue, even at home. Disposable barbecues, or any kind of barbecue, should never be used in the countryside or fields.</li><li>make sure cigarettes are out and dispose of them properly: a single smouldering cigarette butt can reach temperatures of 700C and quickly start a wildfire.</li><li>take litter home – including all bottles and glass. Even small pieces of broken glass can magnify the heat from the sun and cause fires.</li></ul><p>At the first sign of smoke, or if you see a fire, please call 999 immediately and retreat to a safe distance.</p><p>With relatively poor water supply systems in parts of the parish it could be very difficult to put out any fires were they to start …</p><p>Many farm fires happen between spring and late summer due to carelessness by passers-by.</p><p>Please do anything that you can to help avoid fires.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('news','0','2107','2211','en','0','<p>More information about this innovative project, based on land near to the village, was available to everyone at the Buriton Open Gardens Renaturing Trail event.</p><p>Naturum bought the land off Greenway Lane from a local family who had owned it since 2006.&nbsp;</p><p>Previously used for turf cultivation and more recently grazing, Naturum recognised that the site offers good potential for ecological enhancement.</p><p>In the UK, Biodiversity Net Gain (BNG) legislation aims to ensure that new developments leave the natural environment in a measurably better state than beforehand.&nbsp;</p><p>To achieve this, developers must deliver a minimum 10% increase in biodiversity value and maintain those habitat enhancements for at least 30 years.&nbsp;</p><p>Whilst this should be done on the development site whenever possible, off-site locations can be considered.</p><p>With the Buriton site being located close to the Queen Elizabeth Country Park and the Butser Hill National Nature Reserve, the initiative provides opportunities to extend these natural habitats and, in time, integrate the land to create a single thriving natural ecosystem of over 2,000 acres.</p><p>Outline plans for diverse green spaces over the years ahead include:</p><ul><li>planting specialist wildflower and herb seed mixes to enrich grassland biodiversity</li><li>restoring the calcareous grassland found on Butser Hill&nbsp;</li><li>restoring hedgerows to increase species diversity</li><li>extending mixed deciduous woodland areas to support local fauna and invertebrates.</li></ul><p>More information about the initiative can be found in the leaflet attached below.</p>','','full_html'),('news','0','2108','2212','en','0','<p>The Queen Elizabeth Country Park&nbsp;is celebrating its 50th anniversary during August with a free exhibition full of photos from the past, an ‘Adventures Through Time’ children''s trail and nostalgic slices of rhubarb and custard cake in the café as they take things back to the 1970s.&nbsp;</p><p>And they’re always looking to collect favourite memories of the Park.&nbsp;</p><p>Details are on their website <a href="https://www.hants.gov.uk/thingstodo/countryparks/qecp/visit-us/whatson/50th-anniversary-trail?fbclid=IwY2xjawTj5wZwZG9mAWV4dG4DYWVtAjExAHNydGMGYXBwX2lkEDIyMjAzOTE3ODgyMDA4OTIAAR49Z7GHjuydziGk4Mn8oGgAFEyOVlmsGzkyjqfrqraGPJVkVGrtYQTsghKujQ_aem_1wNubsSzQACg80EPi3WZAg">here</a> and on Facebook <a href="https://www.facebook.com/QueenElizabethCP/?locale=en_GB">here</a>.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('news','0','2109','2213','en','0','<p>The work to help teenage girls miss fewer school lessons because of their menstrual periods has already been expanded to five schools in the Dominase area – with two more due to join in September 2026.</p><p>Many girls cannot access or afford expensive products and this can lead to them missing one week in every four of their teenage school career.</p><p>Missing a quarter of their time at school can have a major impact on their educational attainment, confidence and self-esteem and so Buriton’s charity link is trying to help.</p><p>The Village-to-Village Group has been working with ‘Girls Club Ghana’ (a small Ghanaian organisation) and a small team of local nurses who visit each of the schools to help girls learn about menstrual hygiene and encourage the use of relatively inexpensive, locally-made, sustainable period products.&nbsp;</p><p>Washable and re-usable sanitary pads are now been made by local professional seamstresses near to each school, funded by money from Buriton. As well as helping scores of teenage girls, this initiative brings extra income into the local economy and provides skills that are likely to be useful for years to come.</p><p>All the feedback from the project continues to be very positive and constructive.</p><p>Most recently the annual Performance Review report from the main clinic in the area has highlighted a number of achievements from the project: not only the provision of free sanitary pads to school-going children, but also the establishment of valuable ‘adolescent clubs’ at each school and a reduction in teenage pregnancies.</p><p>If the initiative continues to be successful – and if enough money can continue to be raised in Buriton – it’s hoped that it can be extended to all the Junior High Schools in the area over the next few years – helping hundreds of girls every year.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2113','2217','en','0','<p>Tom Cooper plays music by Bach, Schumann and Brahms in St Mary’s church.&nbsp;</p><p>Light refreshments after the concert.</p><p>Retiring collection with all proceeds going to St Mary’s church.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node__body` VALUES ('news','0','2115','2220','en','0','<p>The 2026 ‘Bygone Buriton’ exhibition attracted well over 100 visitors to the village hall in September – with queues to get in at times!</p><p>And a photograph of Harvest Festival activities in the village school in 1956 was one of the items attracting attention.&nbsp;</p><p>Gradually, during the afternoon, eight people of the exhibition at the exhibition realised that they were all pictured in it – and, remarkably, were now reunited and chatting together almost exactly 70 years later.</p><p>All but one of the group now live elsewhere, but had travelled back to Buriton for the event. They are now hoping to track down more of their former schoolmates in time for next year’s exhibition.</p><p>The reunion was just one of many reminders of how much history lies beneath the feet – and in the memories – of Buriton’s residents.</p><p>Thousands of years of the parish’s past were brought to life, from ancient fossils and Bronze Age, Celtic and Roman finds through to the Civil War and beyond. Experts were on hand to explain the stories behind the artefacts and what they reveal about life in Buriton over the centuries.</p><p>Flint arrowheads, Roman finds, thimbles, buttons, buckles, clay pipes, coins and musket balls may all be small, but each has a story to tell, offering fascinating glimpses into the lives of people who lived, worked and travelled through the parish long ago.</p><p>Other displays explored the seasonal hop growing and picking that once transformed the parish, the Buriton and Butser chalk quarries and lime works, and the changes brought by the arrival of the railway.</p><p>The event also marked the 50th anniversary of the Queen Elizabeth Country Park with displays illustrating the history and development of the park, from its opening by Her Late Majesty Queen Elizabeth II, bringing back memories for many visitors.</p><p>As usual the fascinating collection of nearly 100 historic village postcards, some more than a century old, that have all been enlarged to A3 size, was a particular attraction – and more copies of Buriton’s Local History books were snapped up.</p><p>The exhibition attracted visitors from far beyond the parish, including people from Exeter, Windsor, Worthing, Chichester and the New Forest, as well as scores of local residents.</p><p>Buriton’s Heritage Bank has been collecting old photographs, memories and historical information about life in the parish for around 25 years, and yet more were brought to the recent event.</p><p>One of the highlights was some handwritten reflections by a villager who, in 1970, recorded “Buriton: what I remember” while he was in his 80s. His notes provide a fascinating first-hand account of the major changes in family life and work that he had witnessed during his years in the village.</p><p>The organisers are always keen to hear from anyone with old photographs, newspaper cuttings or information relating to Buriton, Weston, Nursted or Ditcham. If you can help with the research in any way, please contact Doug Jones on 01730 231326 or visit <a href="http://www.buriton.org.uk/">www.buriton.org.uk</a></p>','','full_html');
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS `node__comment`;
CREATE TABLE `node__comment` (   `bundle` varchar(128) CHARACTER SET ascii COLLATE ascii_general_ci NOT NULL DEFAULT '' COMMENT 'The field instance bundle to which this row belongs, used when deleting a field instance',   `deleted` tinyint(4) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 COMMENT 'A boolean indicating whether this data item has been deleted',   `entity_id` int(10) unsigned NOT NULL COMMENT 'The entity id this data is attached to',   `revision_id` int(10) unsigned NOT NULL COMMENT 'The entity revision id this data is attached to',   `langcode` varchar(32) CHARACTER SET ascii COLLATE ascii_general_ci NOT NULL DEFAULT '' COMMENT 'The language code for this data item.',   `delta` int(10) unsigned NOT NULL COMMENT 'The sequence number for this data item, used for multi-value fields',   `comment_status` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 COMMENT 'Whether comments are allowed on this entity: 0 = no, 1 = closed (read only), 2 = open (read/write).',   PRIMARY KEY (`entity_id`,`deleted`,`delta`,`langcode`),   KEY `bundle` (`bundle`),   KEY `revision_id` (`revision_id`) ) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 COLLATE=utf8mb4_general_ci COMMENT='Data storage for node field comment.';
