INSERT INTO `node_revision__body` VALUES ('event','0','2070','2166','en','0','<p>The first Saturday of the month means that it’s time to pop into the village hall for the popular Repair Café with skilled sewers, electrical bods, general repairers and computer advice.&nbsp;</p><p>Successful repairs have included:&nbsp;kettles, jumpers, table lamps,&nbsp;garden tools, kitchen knives, wonky tables, picture frames, sports bags and more.&nbsp;</p><p>As well as repairs, many IT queries have been sorted: helping people keep their technology working better and for longer, or giving advice on the best courses of action.&nbsp;</p><p>If you have something that you would like advice on, or something that you’re not sure if it could be repaired, bring it along and the team of repairers will see what can be done.</p><p>With all the usual hot drinks, delicious cakes and warm welcome from the monthly coffee morning team.</p>','','full_html'),('news','0','2071','2167','en','0','<p>The audience in the village hall was treated to a fascinating illustrated talk in March about one of the parish’s most remarkable natural treasures: our dark skies.</p><p>Elinor Newman of the South Downs National Park Authority — affectionately known as the “Queen of Darkness” — gave a lively and enlightening presentation about Buriton’s night skies and the many creatures that emerge after sunset but are rarely seen.</p><p>She reminded everyone that the South Downs became an International Dark Sky Reserve in May 2016, following three years of dedicated work, and that Buriton holds a special place within the Reserve, with two Dark Sky Discovery Sites right on our doorstep.&nbsp;</p><p>Few places offer such a wonderful opportunity to enjoy nature’s finest free spectacle: sparkling stars, bright planets and the sudden thrill of a shooting star streaking overhead.</p><p>The talk also shone a light on the rich nocturnal wildlife that depends on darkness, from bats and owls to glow worms, moths, hedgehogs, frogs and toads. Elinor shared a host of memorable facts about these species, explaining how darkness supports feeding, movement, mating and migration.</p><p>A key message was that darkness matters for wildlife as much as daylight does. Light pollution can disrupt life cycles, disturb feeding corridors and even affect daytime creatures by depriving them of proper rest.</p><p>Encouragingly, there are simple steps everyone can take to help: close curtains, switch lights off when not needed, use modest wattages, and ensure outdoor lighting is downward-facing, shielded and only on when necessary.</p><p>Those attending left both entertained and inspired, with practical ideas for helping protect Buriton’s special night-time environment.</p><p>More tips (and more information about star-gazing) can be found in a special leaflet produced for Buriton: <a href="http://www.buriton.info/sites/default/files/2017-09/Dark%20Skies%20-%20Buriton%20information%20leaflet.pdf">Explore the Dark Side of Buriton</a>&nbsp;</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node_revision__body` VALUES ('event','0','2073','2169','en','0','<p>Buriton’s Local History project has been collecting old photographs, memories and other information about life in the parish for many years.&nbsp;</p><p>This exhibition is a chance to see some of what has been discovered – and perhaps help add another piece to the story.</p><p>There will be a wide range of displays, photographs and information about life in the parish in years gone by – and copies of all Buriton’s Local History books will also be available.</p><p>The exhibition is free and will take place in the village hall on Saturday 26 September, from 1pm to 4pm.</p><p>This year marks the 50th anniversary of the Queen Elizabeth Country Park, and the&nbsp;exhibition will include information about the history of the park, including its opening by Her Late Majesty Queen Elizabeth II.</p><p>And there will be evidence of the thousands of years of history that lie under our feet – from the Bronze Age, Celtic and Roman times through to the Civil War and beyond. &nbsp;The exhibition will include an intriguing collection of finds uncovered by local historians which give clues about what life was like around Buriton long ago. Ancient coins, brooches, buckles, thimbles and buttons may be small, but each offers a glimpse into the lives of people who lived, worked and travelled through the area centuries ago.</p><p>Other displays will include:</p><ul type="disc"><li>Hop growing and picking – the seasonal activity that once transformed the parish.</li><li>The Buriton and Butser chalk quarries and lime works – a reminder of another important local industry.</li><li>The railway – including local families who worked on the line.</li><li>Leisure and entertainment – from football and cricket to pheasant and rabbit shoots, early entertainments and social clubs.</li></ul><p>A particular highlight will be a fascinating collection of nearly 100 historic village postcards, some more than a century old, all enlarged to A3 size.</p><p>Entry is free and everyone is encouraged to bring any old photos, newspaper articles or other information relating to Buriton, Weston, Nursted or Ditcham.</p><p>So come along, explore the displays, share your memories and see how much you recognise. &nbsp;It promises to be another fascinating trip down memory lane!</p><p>If you can help with the research in any way, please contact Doug Jones on 01730 231326 or visit <a href="http://www.buriton.org.uk/">www.buriton.org.uk</a></p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node_revision__body` VALUES ('event','0','2074','2170','en','0','<p>Another wonderful concert in St Mary''s church.</p><p>Music for piano by Franz Schubert</p><p>Played by Tom Cooper</p><p>There will be light refreshments after the concert</p><p>And a retiring collection in aid of the work of St Mary''s</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2078','2175','en','0','<p>Stroll round the village to see how the community is helping nature.</p><p>An opportunity to visit a range of gardens and public open spaces&nbsp;around the village to see how they are catering for wildlife.</p><p>Each garden will show some simple renaturing initiatives to help creatures and the planet. Ask each of the hosts for details.</p><p>There are lots of wonderful plants and creations to see - as well as Cream Teas and Cakes at St Mary''s church (2-5pm).&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>Details of the trail (with a map showing all the locations) will be available from the church.</p><p>It’s hoped that the gardens will inspire everyone to join the community’s campaign to support nature and improve life for all just by doing some very simple things in your own back yard.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2079','2176','en','0','<p>The first Cream Tea of the year in the church.</p><p>All proceeds in aid of St Mary''s.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2085','2182','en','0','<p>The next in the series of coffee mornings will be held in the Village Hall from 10.30am to 12.30.&nbsp;</p><p>Everybody is guaranteed a warm welcome!&nbsp;</p><p>Hot drinks, biscuits and cake will be provided for a small contribution to cover costs (suggested donations: £1 for a drink and £1- £1.50 for a piece of cake).&nbsp;</p><p>If you’d like to bake biscuits or a cake, please let Daniela Jones know (<a href="mailto:daniela.lynchets@gmail.com">daniela.lynchets@gmail.com</a>) and you can easily be reimbursed for the ingredients.</p><p>These coffee mornings are being held on the first Saturday of every month for a chance to see old friends and meet new people.&nbsp;</p><p>It might be especially nice for newcomers who have not had many chances to meet other villagers yet.&nbsp;</p><p>So, please come and do spread the word!&nbsp;</p><p>As parking is limited, please come on foot if at all possible.</p>','','full_html');
INSERT INTO `node_revision__body` VALUES ('event','0','2086','2183','en','0','<p>The next in the series of coffee mornings will be held in the Village Hall from 10.30am to 12.30.&nbsp;</p><p>Everybody is guaranteed a warm welcome!&nbsp;</p><p>Hot drinks, biscuits and cake will be provided for a small contribution to cover costs (suggested donations: £1 for a drink and £1- £1.50 for a piece of cake).&nbsp;</p><p>If you’d like to bake biscuits or a cake, please let Daniela Jones know (<a href="mailto:daniela.lynchets@gmail.com">daniela.lynchets@gmail.com</a>) and you can easily be reimbursed for the ingredients.</p><p>These coffee mornings are being held on the first Saturday of every month for a chance to see old friends and meet new people.&nbsp;</p><p>It might be especially nice for newcomers who have not had many chances to meet other villagers yet.&nbsp;</p><p>So, please come and do spread the word!&nbsp;</p><p>As parking is limited, please come on foot if at all possible.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2087','2184','en','0','<p>The first Saturday of the month means that it’s time to pop into the village hall for the popular Repair Café with skilled sewers, electrical bods, general repairers and computer advice.&nbsp;</p><p>Successful repairs have included:&nbsp;kettles, jumpers, table lamps,&nbsp;garden tools, kitchen knives, wonky tables, picture frames, sports bags and more.&nbsp;</p><p>As well as repairs, many IT queries have been sorted: helping people keep their technology working better and for longer, or giving advice on the best courses of action.&nbsp;</p><p>If you have something that you would like advice on, or something that you’re not sure if it could be repaired, bring it along and the team of repairers will see what can be done.</p><p>With all the usual hot drinks, delicious cakes and warm welcome from the monthly coffee morning team.</p>','','full_html'),('event','0','2088','2185','en','0','<p>The next in the series of coffee mornings will be